6272064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129213 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1045 "Connections Between Matrix Representations of a Linear Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186129967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1050 1200.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129357 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "df9a25dc-813f-52ef-bbe9-65001555ffea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129641 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129641 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129641 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129641 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ef316a2-414a-55cd-b85b-e6597918be4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129641 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129701 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129701 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129701 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129701 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129701 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba0e0a4a-0049-5942-9e25-8da63ac0f245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129701 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 7-5\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13 & 9 \\\\ -16 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129967 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129967 #edb/ref 1061 20144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129967 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "997bad3a-19a8-5b8c-ba06-b960fea4fabc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129967 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129968 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129968 #edb/ref 1061 20145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129968 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9f19301-a016-578d-9241-cc7262d6f30f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129968 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129969 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129969 #edb/ref 1061 20146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129969 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22fcb777-1e74-5081-b738-034b4f36efdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129969 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129970 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129970 #edb/ref 1061 20147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129970 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec690891-eb4f-5c61-8dfc-bd14969e4bcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129970 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130010 #edb/ref 1061 21621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130010 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6ae143c-69c0-50ab-a964-29c6f8f34d83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130010 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130108 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130108 #edb/ref 1061 31802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130108 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1481fd15-5d4d-5662-9141-ebd88670e6fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130108 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130348 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the matrices $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T} : V \\to V$ relative to two bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ of a vector space $V$ are different.\n\nIf we are given the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ how do we find the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}?$\nTo find $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ we use the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nThe diagram below is a commutative diagram that shows how a change of basis works at each step.\n\n![](images/df/dff02d9f46eb7ded23f418765a50dd1971145cd0e585a82beeef1a45812df8a7.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. So algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor instance, if ![](images/89/89530292f17bb402acb489d90a17e7628a33bab37165b02580479b116e6a2b23.svg) and ![](images/f7/f75100cf6de9ccdb537d685f4e2858278214bbe351c9828e8a94a58628c1151b.svg), we use this formula to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the formula for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Alternatively, if we know the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\to {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}$ from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B},$ we can construct a similar diagram:\n\n![](images/d6/d6cd195ccd42804ea91debabd23563bb0f6903fdd1b5d708b712c75417fff2a9.png)\n\nFrom this, we can derive the equation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} =\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130348 #edb/ref 1065 3585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130348 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "daf756b8-28df-5435-a304-0c32de77cc02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130348 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130349 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\, \\ldots, \\, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\, \\ldots, \\, \\mathbf{c}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}.$\nThen the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} =P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change of basis matrix from ${\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nWhen written in expanded form, this formula becomes:\n\n![](images/0c/0c782638ef4254884c9521b8c36