B\\to C}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360747 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63a8e650-529a-5d09-8a67-87993316a8b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360747 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452994 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452995 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452996 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360748 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360748 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360748 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47f484a7-fae0-59b3-b230-0181ae09e78c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360748 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/9b/9ba3d1f0eebdfc82c40f36bdbe16896ca15516b623c695b72c331861955329bc.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452997 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452998 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452999 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360749 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360749 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360749 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360749 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "977acc9f-1a7e-5840-a4f9-507f36de32d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360749 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/9b/9ba3d1f0eebdfc82c40f36bdbe16896ca15516b623c695b72c331861955329bc.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360755 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360755 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360755 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360755 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360755 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18ca38eb-405c-519d-860c-9cd4de1b59e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360755 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360776 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360776 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360776 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360776 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360776 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c1ef303-e889-5fc1-8e48-5dba05ad5f94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360776 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 &